            <ArticleTitle>Exercise-induced respiratory symptoms: an epidemic among adolescents.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>361-7; quiz 368-70, 412</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.anai.2009.12.008</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To review the most important causes of exercise-induced respiratory symptoms in adolescents.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DATA SOURCES" NlmCategory="METHODS">Published English-language medical literature.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="STUDY SELECTION" NlmCategory="METHODS">Primary literature and consensus publications relevant to the objective.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The diagnosis and treatment of exercise-induced asthma are well characterized. Other syndromes, such as exercise-induced vocal cord dysfunction, exercise-induced paradoxical arytenoid motion, and exercise-induced hyperventilation, are relatively common but so far are described primarily in uncontrolled case reports.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Controlled studies are necessary to define efficient diagnostic and treatment algorithms for young patients experiencing respiratory symptoms during exercise who do not respond to asthma treatment.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
